    "description": "ProDough's plant protein lists 25 g of protein and 120 to 150 calories per serving with zero added sugar, in Brownie Batter, Cafe Mocha, Salted Caramel and Vanilla Cone, per its page at the time of writing. It was listed as sold out when we checked, and the page text did not name the plant protein sources or servings per bag. A store reply on the page put sodium at around 250 mg per scoop for most flavors.",
    "pros": [
      "25 g of protein per serving, per the brand",
      "Dessert flavors not common in plant powders",
      "Dairy-free"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Sold out at the time of writing",
      "Protein sources and serving count not stated in page text"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Check the panel and stock before buying; better-documented plant proteins are easy to find.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "25 g (brand)",
      "Calories": "About 120-150",
      "Third-party testing": "None named",
      "Best for": "Plant-based dessert shakes"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "transparent-labs-grass-fed-whey-protein-isolate",
    "name": "Transparent Labs 100% Grass-Fed Whey Protein Isolate (Unflavored)",
    "brand": "Transparent Labs",
    "badge": "Alternative: Documented Grass-Fed Isolate",
    "category": "Grass-fed whey isolate",
    "description": "If what appeals about ProDough is a grass-fed whey isolate, Transparent Labs gives you one with more protein per scoop and published third-party lab results. The unflavored version delivers about 28 g of protein per scoop with no sweeteners or flavors, per the label at the time of writing, which also makes it a far more flexible baking ingredient. It is sold on Amazon.",
    "pros": [
      "About 28 g of protein per scoop, per the label",
      "No sweeteners or flavors, so it bakes cleanly",
      "Brand publishes third-party lab results"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Premium cost per 25 g of protein",
      "Unflavored availability varies; check the current flavor list"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The better grass-fed isolate if you value documentation over dessert flavors.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "About 28 g (label)",
      "Calories": "Check panel",
      "Third-party testing": "Published lab results",
      "Best for": "Baking, maximum protein per scoop"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "naked-nutrition-naked-whey",
    "name": "Naked Nutrition Naked Whey (Unflavored)",
    "brand": "Naked Nutrition",
    "badge": "Alternative: Best for Baking",
    "category": "Grass-fed whey concentrate",
    "description": "Naked Whey is the benchmark single-ingredient unflavored whey: grass-fed whey protein concentrate and nothing else, per the label. It delivers about 25 g of protein per two-scoop serving and buyers consistently describe it as mild and milky, easy to hide in oats, smoothies and baking. In large bags it costs far less per 25 g than ProDough, which makes it the sensible base for homemade protein baking.",
    "pros": [
      "One ingredient: grass-fed whey protein concentrate, per the label",
      "About 25 g of protein per two-scoop serving",
      "Mid-range cost per 25 g in large bags"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Concentrate contains some lactose",
      "Unflavored, so it needs a recipe to taste of anything"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The default unflavored whey for baking and cooking at a fraction of ProDough's cost.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "About 25 g (2 scoops, label)",
      "Calories": "Check panel",
      "Third-party testing": "Verify current status",
      "Best for": "Baking, oats, smoothies"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "ProDough Protein Review",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/prodough-protein-powder-review/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is ProDough protein powder good?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "ProDough's whey is a credible product: the brand describes it as grass-fed whey isolate with 25 g of protein and 110 calories per serving, in a long list of dessert flavors buyers rate highly. Its drawbacks are a premium price around 3 dollars per 25 g, no named third-party certification and an undisclosed sweetener in its page text."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does ProDough actually make protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Although ProDough is best known for protein pancake, crepe, cupcake and donut mixes, it sells three protein powders at the time of writing: a grass-fed whey isolate, a grass-fed beef protein with collagen peptides in stick packs, and a plant protein. Each lists 25 g of protein per serving, per the brand."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much protein is in a scoop of ProDough whey?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "ProDough lists 25 g of protein, 110 calories and 2 g of net carbs per serving of its whey isolate, with zero added sugar, per its page at the time of writing. The published text did not give the serving weight in grams, so check the label image for the exact scoop size and full panel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is ProDough protein sold on Amazon?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "We could not confirm an official ProDough listing on Amazon at the time of writing. The brand sells primarily through its own online store, where it lists Subscribe and Save discounts and free shipping over 100 dollars. Third-party resellers have listed some ProDough mixes, but availability and freshness are harder to verify that way."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is ProDough protein third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "ProDough's product pages that we checked did not mention a third-party testing program or certification such as NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or the Clean Label Project. That does not mean the products are unsafe, but there is no public database to check, so drug-tested athletes should choose a certified powder instead."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you bake with ProDough protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can, and buyers report using it in oats, yogurt and protein ice cream. But flavored, sweetened protein at around 3 dollars per 25 g is an expensive baking ingredient. Most home bakers get better value and more flexibility from a large bag of unflavored whey, replacing part of the flour and adding moisture."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is ProDough whey lactose-free?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "ProDough describes its whey as isolate rather than concentrate, which typically contains very little lactose, and adds a digestive enzyme blend. It does not claim the product is lactose-free. Its beef protein is fully dairy-free. If you are lactose intolerant or allergic to milk, check the label and speak to a doctor before using whey."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "prodough protein powder",
      "prodough protein",
      "prodough review",
      "prodough protein baking mix"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Unflavored Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-unflavored-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">ProDough Protein Powder: A Bakeshop Brand With a Whey Isolate</Fragment>
      ProDough started with protein donut and pancake mixes and now sells its own whey isolate, beef protein and plant protein in dessert flavors. Here is what the labels actually say, which product to buy, who should skip it, and what it costs per 25 g of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#what-prodough-sells">What ProDough Actually Sells</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in ProDough Whey</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Lineup</a>
      <a href="#which-one">Which ProDough Product to Buy</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#transparency">Testing and Label Transparency</a>
      <a href="#skip">Who Should Skip ProDough</a>
      <a href="#baking">Baking With Protein: ProDough vs Unflavored</a>
      <a href="#compare">How ProDough Compares</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          ProDough's whey is a 25 g grass-fed whey isolate in about 110 calories, sold in an unusually long list of dessert flavors. It is also around 2.95 dollars per 25 g of protein, sold only direct as far as we could confirm, and published without a named sweetener, a serving weight or a third-party certification. Buy it for the flavors; buy something else for value or for baking.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            ProDough is a real brand with a real protein powder, which is worth saying because many people know it only for its protein pancake and donut mixes. Its whey protein is described by the brand as 100 percent microfiltered, grass-fed whey isolate with 25 g of protein and 110 calories per serving, 2 g of net carbs and zero added sugar. That is a good macro profile, roughly in line with premium isolates from mainstream brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            The draw is flavor. ProDough lists around 19 flavors on its whey page, from Vanilla Bean and Chocolate Mousse to Raspberry Cheesecake, Horchata, Maple Donut and Orange Cream, and refers to more than 30 elsewhere. Buyer reviews on the brand's review platform overwhelmingly focus on taste, with frequent mentions of mixing it into coffee, overnight oats, yogurt and protein ice cream. If dessert flavors keep you consistent with protein, that has real value.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weaknesses are price and disclosure. A full bag lists at about 60 dollars, which ProDough itself prices at about 2.95 dollars per serving, or about 2.95 dollars per 25 g of protein, since each serving holds 25 g. That is premium pricing. And the published product text we checked did not name the sweetener, give a serving weight in grams or name any third-party certification, despite the brand's tagline promising transparent labels. The panel is published as an image, so check it before you buy.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Good macros and fun flavors, priced like a premium isolate, with less disclosure than one.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy ProDough whey if flavor variety is what keeps you using protein and you are happy to pay around 3 dollars per 25 g. If you want protein for baking, an unflavored isolate is the better and cheaper tool. If you need certification, look elsewhere.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            This review is built on ProDough's own product pages, retailer and wholesale listings, and buyer reviews reported as buyer feedback. We have not tasted, baked with or lab-tested ProDough products ourselves. For the unflavored powders most bakers end up using instead, see our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">best unflavored protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-prodough-sells" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What ProDough Actually Sells
          </h2>

          <p>
            ProDough trades as ProDough Protein Bakeshop and sells primarily through its own online store. Its wholesale listing describes a line of high-protein, low-sugar baked goods and pantry staples, from protein cupcakes to gluten-free flour and breakfast options, and its site carries the tagline Clean Ingredients, Great Macros, Transparent Labels. The review platform attached to its store showed more than 7,000 buyer reviews at the time of writing, which suggests a sizeable direct customer base.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein powder side of the range has three products. The whey protein is the flagship, sold in full-size bags and as Protein Singles, single-serve packs that the brand also bundles as a sampler with one serving of every flavor. A grass-fed beef protein is sold in single-serve stick packs in four flavors. A plant protein is sold in full bags in four flavors, and was listed as sold out when we checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bakeshop side is larger. At the time of writing ProDough sold protein crepe mixes in Original and Chocolate, Buttermilk and Cinnamon Swirl Protein Pancake and Waffle Mixes, cupcake mixes in flavors such as Chocolate, Cinnamon Swirl and Pumpkin Spice, protein donut mixes, a gluten-free one-to-one flour blend, protein oatmeal quick cups and a greens powder. Baking mixes run about 12 to 15 dollars a bag at list price on the brand's site.
          </p>

          <p>
            Amazon is the awkward part for this site's readers. We could not confirm an official ProDough storefront or listing on Amazon at the time of writing. One buyer on ProDough's review page mentioned previously buying on Amazon, and a third-party grocery reseller listed the buttermilk pancake mix as sold out. If you want ProDough, the brand's own store is the reliable source, and the Amazon buttons on this page for ProDough items may lead to a search rather than a product page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in ProDough Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            ProDough's whey page makes a handful of specific claims: 100 percent microfiltered whey isolate, described as isolate, not concentrate; 100 percent grass-fed; 25 g of protein and 110 calories per serving; 2 g of net carbs; zero added sugar; and zero artificial flavors, colors or preservatives. It also describes the powder as gluten-free and keto-friendly and says it includes a proprietary digestive enzyme blend.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those macros are credible for a whey isolate. Isolate is typically around 90 percent protein by weight, and the brand claims more than 90 percent protein per scoop, which would put a 25 g serving at roughly 27 to 28 g of powder. That would be a normal scoop for an isolate. Isolate also contains much less lactose and fat than concentrate, which is why many people with mild lactose sensitivity tolerate it better.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the page text does not tell you is the sweetener. ProDough says zero added sugar and no artificial flavors, but the published text we checked did not name whether the whey is sweetened with stevia, monk fruit, sucralose or something else. Its beef protein page, by contrast, clearly lists Reb A and Reb M stevia. The supplement facts for the whey appear only as images, so read the panel on the flavor you are considering before buying, especially if you avoid a particular sweetener.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per serving (brand claims)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Whey Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Beef Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Plant Protein</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120-150</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbohydrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g net</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not stated in text</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grass-fed whey isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grass-fed beef protein plus collagen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plant, sources not stated in text</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not stated in text</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reb A and Reb M stevia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not stated in text</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Proprietary digestive enzyme blends are common in flavored wheys and are hard to evaluate, because the amounts are not disclosed. They are unlikely to cause harm, and some people feel they help with bloating, but they are not a reason on their own to pay more. If digestion is your main concern, the isolate base matters far more than the enzyme blend.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The ProDough Protein Lineup, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are the ProDough protein powders we could confirm on the brand's own site at the time of writing, flagship first, followed by two clearly labelled unflavored alternatives that are sold on Amazon. ProDough sells direct, and we could not confirm an official ProDough listing on Amazon, so the buttons on its own products may lead to a search rather than a product page. Figures are per the brand's published claims at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which ProDough Product to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most readers the answer is the whey. It has the best macros in the range, 25 g of protein in 110 calories, the most flavors and the most buyer feedback. Start with the Protein Singles or the sampler to find two or three flavors you like, then move to full bags with Subscribe and Save, which ProDough lists at 10 percent off. Buying full bags of flavors you have never tried is the quickest way to waste 60 dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            The beef protein is for people who cannot or will not use dairy and do not want a plant protein. ProDough describes it as 100 percent grass-fed beef protein with bovine collagen peptides added for body, at 25 g of protein, 100 calories and 2 g of carbs per stick, sweetened with Reb A and Reb M stevia and flavored with cocoa and natural flavors. It is dairy-free, with no whey, casein or lactose. Note that collagen peptides are an incomplete protein, so a blend that leans on collagen is less efficient for muscle building than the same grams of whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            The plant protein lists 25 g of protein and 120 to 150 calories per serving in Brownie Batter, Cafe Mocha, Salted Caramel and Vanilla Cone, at about 50 dollars a bag. It was sold out when we checked, and the page text did not name the plant protein sources or the serving count, which makes it hard to compare on cost. A store reply on the page said most flavors carry around 250 mg of sodium per scoop. Wait until it is back in stock and check the panel before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            The baking mixes are a separate decision. They are a convenient way to make higher-protein pancakes, crepes, cupcakes and donuts, and buyers report fluffy results from the pancake mix. But the product page text we checked did not list protein per serving for the pancake mix, so compare its panel against a homemade version using unflavored protein powder, which is usually cheaper per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            If beef protein is what interests you, our <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">best beef protein powder</a> guide compares the main options on dose, sourcing and cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because every ProDough powder lists 25 g of protein per serving, its cost per 25 g is simply its cost per serving, which makes the comparison easy. ProDough prices its full-size whey bag at about 60 dollars, which it lists as about 2.95 dollars per serving. Subscribe and Save brings the bag to about 54 dollars, or roughly 2.65 dollars per 25 g. Protein Singles are listed at about 3.95 dollars each.
          </p>

          <p>
            The beef protein Build Your Variety Pack was listed at about 3.49 dollars per stick, so about 3.49 dollars per 25 g of protein. The plant protein bag was listed at about 50 dollars, but without a serving count in the page text we could not convert it reliably. Shipping is free over 100 dollars, which matters for a direct-only brand: a single bag below that threshold costs more than the sticker price suggests.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Approximate cost per 25 g of protein</p>
