          <p>
            For comparison, mainstream unflavored whey in large bags typically lands well under 1.50 dollars per 25 g of protein, and even premium grass-fed isolates from established brands often come in around 2 dollars. ProDough's whey is therefore one of the more expensive ways to buy a gram of whey isolate. You are paying for the flavor range and the brand's recipes, not for the protein itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            These figures are approximate and built from list prices that change, so recheck them before you buy. If you are working out how much a month of protein would cost at your own target, the <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> gives you a daily number to multiply by.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="transparency" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Testing and Label Transparency
          </h2>

          <p>
            ProDough's tagline promises transparent labels, and the headline macros are clearly stated. But the product pages we checked did not mention any third-party testing program or certification, such as NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice, Informed Sport or the Clean Label Project. The whey page says the brand keeps production in-house without naming where the products are made. None of that means the products are unsafe; it means there is no independent record you can check.
          </p>

          <p>
            The missing sweetener disclosure in the page text is the bigger gap for everyday buyers. Many people choose a protein powder specifically to avoid sucralose or stevia, and dessert-flavored powders almost always use one or the other. The answer is likely on the label image, but a brand that leads with transparency should state it in text. If you ask ProDough directly, the store responds to questions on its product pages.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey isolate is generally a low-risk protein source for contaminants, as dairy proteins tend to test lower for heavy metals than plant proteins. That is reassuring for the whey, less so for the plant protein, where independent heavy-metal testing matters more. If contaminant testing is a priority, our guide to whether <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder is safe</a> explains what to look for.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHO NEEDS CERTIFICATION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Drug-tested athletes should not buy an uncertified powder.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you compete in a tested sport, buy a protein that carries NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport and check your lot in the certifier's database. ProDough does not advertise either, so it is the wrong choice for you regardless of flavor.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            As with any supplement, anyone with kidney disease, a milk or other food allergy, or who is pregnant or taking medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding protein powder. Nothing on this page is medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Skip ProDough
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip ProDough if you want protein mainly for baking or cooking. Flavored, sweetened powders limit what you can make, and at around 3 dollars per 25 g they are an expensive ingredient to bake with. An unflavored whey or whey isolate goes into savory and sweet recipes alike, lets you control the sweetener, and costs far less per gram. That is the main reason this review points to unflavored alternatives.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are on a budget or use two shakes a day. At roughly 3 dollars per serving, two shakes a day is close to 180 dollars a month, several times what a large bag of mainstream whey would cost for the same protein. Skip it if you want to buy on Amazon with fast shipping, since we could not confirm an official listing there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you need to know the sweetener before you buy and cannot easily read the label image, or if you need a third-party certification. And skip the beef protein if your goal is maximizing muscle protein synthesis per gram: beef protein isolates and added collagen are lower in leucine than whey, so the same 25 g does less work.
          </p>

          <p>
            ProDough makes the most sense for one kind of buyer: someone who already likes protein but is bored of chocolate and vanilla, uses one serving a day, and values dessert flavors such as Raspberry Cheesecake or Snickerdoodle enough to pay a premium for them. For that buyer, it is a reasonable splurge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="baking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Baking With Protein: ProDough vs Unflavored Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            ProDough's appeal for many buyers is that it takes the guesswork out of protein baking. Its mixes are formulated so that you add water, or milk and an egg for more protein, and cook. That convenience is real. Protein powder behaves differently from flour, and recipes that simply swap one for the other tend to come out dry, rubbery or dense, so a pre-built mix saves a lot of trial and error.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is cost and control. If you bake regularly, an unflavored whey isolate or whey concentrate, mixed into your own recipes, is almost always cheaper per gram of protein, and you choose the sweetener and flavor. A good starting rule is to replace only part of the flour with protein powder, often around a quarter to a third, and add moisture with Greek yogurt, mashed banana, pumpkin or an extra egg.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey and casein behave differently in the oven. Whey can dry out baked goods if overused, while casein and blends tend to hold moisture better, which is why some bakers mix the two. Heating denatures protein, which changes its texture, but it does not destroy its nutritional value: the amino acids are still there and still count toward your intake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant proteins absorb more liquid than whey and tend to taste earthier, so they suit chocolate, spice and banana recipes better than delicate vanilla bakes. Our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">unflavored protein powder guide</a> ranks the plain options for cooking, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">protein powder for smoothies</a> guide covers the blend-and-drink side.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you like the idea of ProDough's flavors but want to control costs, a sensible split is to buy a bag of one or two ProDough flavors you genuinely enjoy for shakes and oats, and use a large bag of unflavored whey for everything you bake. That gets you the variety without paying dessert-brand prices for every gram you put in the oven.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How ProDough Compares
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against Transparent Labs Grass-Fed Whey Protein Isolate, ProDough is the closer match on paper, since both are grass-fed isolates. Transparent Labs lists about 28 g of protein per scoop in its unflavored version, publishes third-party lab results and is sold on Amazon. ProDough offers far more flavors. If you value documented testing, Transparent Labs is the better buy; our <a href="/transparent-labs-protein-powder-review/">Transparent Labs review</a> covers the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against Naked Whey, ProDough loses on price and simplicity and wins on flavor. Naked Whey is grass-fed whey concentrate and nothing else, about 25 g of protein per two-scoop serving, at a mid-range cost per 25 g in large bags. It is the classic baking whey because it has no sweetener or flavor to fight with. Our <a href="/naked-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Naked Nutrition review</a> goes into its full line.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against other flavor-led, direct-to-consumer brands, ProDough's niche is its bakery heritage and dessert flavor range. Brands such as <a href="/flavcity-protein-powder-review/">FlavCity</a> and <a href="/clean-simple-eats-protein-powder-review/">Clean Simple Eats</a> play in similar territory, with flavor-forward powders sold largely to home cooks. If flavor is your main criterion, our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best tasting protein powder</a> guide covers the mainstream options.
          </p>

