          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is ProDough protein powder good?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  ProDough's whey is a credible product: the brand describes it as grass-fed whey isolate with 25 g of protein and 110 calories per serving, in a long list of dessert flavors buyers rate highly. Its drawbacks are a premium price around 3 dollars per 25 g, no named third-party certification and an undisclosed sweetener in its page text.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does ProDough actually make protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Although ProDough is best known for protein pancake, crepe, cupcake and donut mixes, it sells three protein powders at the time of writing: a grass-fed whey isolate, a grass-fed beef protein with collagen peptides in stick packs, and a plant protein. Each lists 25 g of protein per serving, per the brand.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in a scoop of ProDough whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  ProDough lists 25 g of protein, 110 calories and 2 g of net carbs per serving of its whey isolate, with zero added sugar, per its page at the time of writing. The published text did not give the serving weight in grams, so check the label image for the exact scoop size and full panel.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is ProDough protein sold on Amazon?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  We could not confirm an official ProDough listing on Amazon at the time of writing. The brand sells primarily through its own online store, where it lists Subscribe and Save discounts and free shipping over 100 dollars. Third-party resellers have listed some ProDough mixes, but availability and freshness are harder to verify that way.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is ProDough protein third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  ProDough's product pages that we checked did not mention a third-party testing program or certification such as NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or the Clean Label Project. That does not mean the products are unsafe, but there is no public database to check, so drug-tested athletes should choose a certified powder instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you bake with ProDough protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  You can, and buyers report using it in oats, yogurt and protein ice cream. But flavored, sweetened protein at around 3 dollars per 25 g is an expensive baking ingredient. Most home bakers get better value and more flexibility from a large bag of unflavored whey, replacing part of the flour and adding moisture.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is ProDough whey lactose-free?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  ProDough describes its whey as isolate rather than concentrate, which typically contains very little lactose, and adds a digestive enzyme blend. It does not claim the product is lactose-free. Its beef protein is fully dairy-free. If you are lactose intolerant or allergic to milk, check the label and speak to a doctor before using whey.
